{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-k1-reviewer-fable-48","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:e9e2718573bb47f7d17b","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files only: the report's selected statistical conclusion (Welch t = 2.874, df = 45.7, p < 0.01, 'significant at alpha = 0.05') is obtained by entering all four nubbin rows of each of the 12 colonies as separate observations (n = 24 per zone) into Welch's two-sample t-test, a procedure that assumes independent rows, even though the data description designates colony_id as the independent unit. Recomputing the same Welch test on the 12 colony means (n = 6 per zone) yields the same mean difference (0.305) but t ≈ 1.35, df ≈ 10, p ≈ 0.21, so the claimed significance is not supported at the declared independent-unit level.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py's read_densities() appends every CSV row's symbiont_density_e6_per_cm2 into a flat per-zone array with no aggregation or modeling of colony_id, and build_report() passes those 24-element arrays directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind(..., equal_var=False). Because each colony supplied four nubbins that are near-duplicates (within-colony SD ≈ 0.03–0.07 vs between-colony SD of colony means ≈ 0.40), each independent unit enters the row-independent t-test four times, inflating the per-group sample size from 6 to 24 and the degrees of freedom from ~10 to ~46, which manufactures the p < 0.01 result.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description explicitly names colony_id as the independent-unit column and states that one row is a nubbin, with each colony appearing on four rows.","observed_value":"independent unit = colony_id; 12 colonies x 4 rows each = 48 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":12,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"One row is: one nubbin chipped from a tagged coral colony, together with the endosymbiont density counted for that nubbin\nIndependent unit column: colony_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":17,"end_line":20,"quoted_text":"- `colony_id`: tag code of the coral colony the nubbin came from. Twelve\n  colonies were tagged (LG-01 to LG-06 in the lagoon, FR-01 to FR-06 on the\n  forereef) and each one supplied four nubbins, so each code appears on four\n  rows."}]},{"description":"The workflow reads every CSV row into a flat per-zone list keyed only by reef_zone, with no reference to colony_id anywhere in the script, then runs a row-independent Welch two-sample t-test on those arrays.","observed_value":"48 rows -> two arrays of 24; scipy.stats.ttest_ind(lagoon, forereef, equal_var=False)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":26,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"    with path.open(newline=\"\", encoding=\"ascii\") as handle:\n        for record in csv.DictReader(handle):\n            zone = record[ZONE_FIELD].strip()\n            buckets[zone].append(float(record[DENSITY_FIELD]))"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":53,"end_line":53,"quoted_text":"    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(lagoon, forereef, equal_var=False)"}]},{"description":"The report confirms that every nubbin row was an observation in the test and reports n = 24 per zone with df = 45.7, i.e., each colony (independent unit) entered the test four times.","observed_value":"n = 24 per zone; df = 45.7; p < 0.01","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"Endosymbiont density (millions of cells per cm^2) was compared between the two\nreef zones with Welch's two-sample t-test (scipy.stats.ttest_ind with\nequal_var=False). Every nubbin record in data/input.csv contributed one\nobservation to the test."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":14,"end_line":15,"quoted_text":"| lagoon | 24 | 1.833 | 0.382 |\n| forereef | 24 | 1.528 | 0.353 |"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"Welch t = 2.874, df = 45.7, p < 0.01 (two-sided, alpha = 0.05)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":20,"end_line":20,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Lagoon nubbins carry a higher endosymbiont density than forereef nubbins: mean difference 0.305 million cells per cm^2 (1.833 vs 1.528), Welch t = 2.874, df = 45.7, p < 0.01, so the zone difference is significant at alpha = 0.05."}]},{"description":"The rows within a colony are near-duplicates, so the repeated entries are strongly dependent: e.g., LG-01's four rows span only 2.04–2.15 while colony means across the dataset span 1.08–2.42. The description itself warns of this dependence.","observed_value":"within-colony SD ~0.03–0.07 vs between-colony SD of colony means ~0.40","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"LG-01,lagoon,LG-01-N1,3.4,2.04\nLG-01,lagoon,LG-01-N2,3.4,2.08\nLG-01,lagoon,LG-01-N3,3.4,2.13\nLG-01,lagoon,LG-01-N4,3.4,2.15"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":30,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"Nubbins taken from the same colony share the same genotype, the same light and\nflow history and the same handling batch, so their densities are much more\nalike than densities from different colonies."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report is transparent about its unit handling: it discloses that every nubbin record contributed one observation, so the repeated entry is not concealed. This mitigates concerns of misreporting but does not make the significance claim valid.","observed_value":"methodology disclosed in report","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":7,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"equal_var=False). Every nubbin record in data/input.csv contributed one\nobservation to the test."}]},{"description":"The descriptive part of the claim survives correct aggregation: because the design is balanced (4 nubbins per colony), the zone means and their difference of 0.305 are identical whether computed over nubbins or over colony means, and the direction (lagoon higher) is unchanged. Only the inferential significance statement is invalidated.","observed_value":"mean difference 0.305 at both nubbin and colony level; colony-level p ≈ 0.21","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":17,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"Mean difference (lagoon minus forereef): 0.305 million cells per cm^2."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defend the report in three ways. (1) Checked for a computation error that might explain the result instead: recomputing the row-level Welch test by hand from the CSV gives means 1.833 vs 1.528, SDs 0.382 and 0.353, t = 0.305/sqrt(0.382^2/24 + 0.353^2/24) ≈ 2.87, matching the reported 2.874 — the code faithfully implements what the report describes, so the result is fully attributable to the unit choice, not a bug. (2) Tested whether the conclusion is robust to correct unit handling: aggregating to the 12 colony means (lagoon: 2.10, 1.55, 2.42, 1.28, 1.94, 1.71; forereef: 1.62, 1.19, 2.05, 1.40, 1.08, 1.83) and rerunning Welch's test gives the same mean difference 0.305 but SE ≈ 0.226, t ≈ 1.35, Welch df ≈ 10, two-sided p ≈ 0.21 — not significant, so the p < 0.01 claim does not survive. (3) Considered whether nubbins could plausibly be treated as independent despite the description: the data contradict this — within-colony spreads (~0.03–0.07) are an order of magnitude smaller than the spread of colony means (~0.40), indicating near-total within-colony correlation, and the description explicitly designates colony_id as the independent unit. All three defenses fail, confirming the issue.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}